      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "Platform-specific (macOS). The likely cause is the harness, not Curl's ECH client. With an ecl: config list, HandBuiltTlsProvider.HandshakeAsync (Curl.Networking.UnitLibrary) offers TLS 1.3 only (settings.EchConfigs is not null, so runsTls12 is false; BL-1950), as curl's OpenSSL build does. The harness's HTTPS stand-in, Curl.Conformance.UnitLibrary/TlsServerStream.cs, is an SslStream server, and on macOS .NET's server-side SslStream negotiates at most TLS 1.2. The handshake therefore fails on the protocol version (exit 35) before the client can see that the server ignored the ECH offer and send ech_required (exit 101). Change Curl.Conformance.UnitLibrary so its HTTPS server speaks TLS 1.3 on macOS, as upstream's stunnel/OpenSSL server does: for example, serve TLS through Curl's hand-built TLS 1.3 server code (Curl.Tls.UnitLibrary) when the platform's SslStream server cannot. Add a unit test in Curl.Conformance.UnitTests that the macOS HTTPS stand-in completes a TLS 1.3-only ClientHello. Then rerun test4001 on macOS. If it still exits 35, check Tls13ClientHandshake's ech_required decision (TlsAlertDescription.EchRequired) against a server that ignores the ECH extension, and add a focused test in Curl.Tls.UnitTests (ADR-0482). Explained by ADR-0327 (ECH runs only on the hand-built client) and ADR-0359 (exit 101 text as measured with OpenSSL); neither explains the macOS-only exit 35.",
